{# Light or dark, in the corner rather than the header.

   A circle the size of "back to top" and wearing the same border, surface and
   shadow, because the two are now one stack and a pill beside a circle would
   read as two unrelated things. Which of them is on top is not fixed: this one
   sits at the bottom while there is nothing to scroll back to, and lifts above
   the other when it appears -- so the control nearest the thumb is always the
   one that is always there.

   The icon is what you will get, not what you have: a moon means "make it
   dark". That is the convention almost everywhere, and it is the one that
   survives being seen without a label.

   `aria-pressed` on a plain button, rather than `role="switch"`: there is no
   track to look at, and "Dark theme, pressed" is what this is. The markup ships
   unpressed for everybody because the same HTML goes to every reader -- the
   head script has already applied the real palette and global.js corrects the
   button. #}
<button type="button" class="theme-toggle js-theme" aria-pressed="false">
    <span class="theme-toggle__icons" aria-hidden="true">
        <i class="fas fa-moon theme-toggle__moon"></i>
        <i class="fas fa-sun theme-toggle__sun"></i>
    </span>
    <span class="visually-hidden">Dark theme</span>
</button>
